{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfpmksYgxsDDk45h2XmkR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Implementation is in `src/str.js:7-14`, full suite green at 9/9.\n\n```js\nfunction slugify(s) {\n  return s\n    .normalize('NFD')\n    .replace(/[\\u0300-\\u036f]/g, '')  // strip combining marks\n    .toLowerCase()\n    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')\n    .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, '');\n}\n```\n\nThe explicit trim from the issue is handled implicitly — leading/trailing whitespace becomes a dash and then gets stripped by the last step, so a separate `trim()` call would be dead code.\n\n**The probe found a real gap, and it's exactly the failure mode we were just discussing.** Green tests, partial interpretation:\n\n| input | output | wanted |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Søren` | `s-ren` | `soren` |\n| `Straße` | `stra-e` | `strasse` |\n| `Łódź` | `odz` | `lodz` |\n| `Đorđe` | `or-e` | `dorde` |\n\nNFD only decomposes characters that are *base + accent*. Letters with a stroke through them (`ø ł đ`) and ligatures (`ß`) are atomic code points — they don't decompose, so they fall through to `[^a-z0-9]` and become dashes. My three test cases (`café`, `naïve`, `crème`) are all the decomposable kind, so they sailed through and told me nothing about this. If I'd only looked at the suite I'd have reported this as finished.\n\nNon-Latin scripts collapse to `''` entirely (`日本語` → `\"\"`). That one I'd call correct-by-design for an ASCII slug, not a bug.\n\nFixing the stroke/ligature cases needs an explicit transliteration map — roughly a dozen entries for common European letters, or a dependency for full coverage. That's past what the issue asked for, so I've left it alone rather than quietly widening scope. Say the word and it's a few minutes.\n\nNothing is committed — working tree has both files modified."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_